        "id": "ch1_3-1",
        "text": "Strategizing the development process\nStrategizing the development process is about planning each of the phases and looking\ninto the process flow from one phase to another. To strategize the development process,\nwe need to first answer the following questions:\n1.I Are we looking for a minimal design approach and going straight to the coding\nphase with little design?\n2.I Do we want test-driven development ( TDD ), whereby we first create tests using\nthe requirements and then code them?\n3.I Do we want to create a minimum viable product ( MVP ) first and iteratively evolve\nthe solution?\n4.I What is the strategy for validating NFRs such as security and performance?\n5.I Are we looking for a single-node development, or do we want to develop and deploy\non the cluster or in the cloud?\n6.I What are the volume, velocity, and variety of our input and output ( I/O ) data?\nIs it a Hadoop distributed file system ( HDFS ) or Simple Storage Service ( S3 )\nfile-based structure, or a Structured Query Language ( SQL ) or NoSQL database?\nIs the data on-premises or in the cloud?\n7.I Are we working on specialized use cases such as ML with specific requirements for\ncreating data pipelines, testing models, and deploying and maintaining them?\nBased on the answers to these questions, we can strategize the steps for our development\nprocess. In more recent times, it is always preferred to use iterative development processes\nin one form or another. The concept of MVP as a starting goal is also popular. We will\ndiscuss these in the next subsections, along with the domains' specific development needs.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Iterating through the phases\n\u2022 Aiming for MVP first\n\u2022 Strategizing development for specialized domains\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
